title The box nobody opened, and the probe that would have said it was fine
prompt The baton, BAKERY's seat -- core infrastructure Lindy-first and crux-first.
think Lap N is 4035 by git rev-list --count HEAD, so the rota row is 0 -- Aether, Jupiter, the row that hears: why the work exists. The previous BAKERY lap read row 4, so this advances by one. Reading through that sense picked the lap: `%464` is a record channel with a hole in it, and a channel exists to be heard on. The compass's own question -- would this still matter if the code were deleted -- answers itself for a session log, which IS the thing that survives the code.
think Reds first pointed at `%464` over my own recommend line. Nine rows stand OPEN; `%464` is the one in my lane (`tools/f/fleet_round_open.sh` is fleet infrastructure, and the fleet's own friction is this seat's named second duty), it is agent-closable, and its row names the instrument it wants in one sentence. The recommend line's scope-map pass is real work and keeps.
think I measured before building, and the measurement was worse than the row. The row recorded three stashes at `20260906.043823`; four stand now, and three of the four hold a session log nothing else carries -- `20260906.055857`, `20260906.045131`, `20260906.011125`. All three are this ship's own laps. One is the lap that landed the nib writer, so that code shipped and its reasoning stayed in the box for two hours.
obs The probe I reached for first was wrong, and finding that out is most of what this lap is worth. `git log --all --oneline -- '*20260906-055857*'` answers ONE commit, `98b56e594`, which reads like history. It is `stash@{0}^2` -- the stash's own index commit. `--all` includes `refs/stash`, so the obvious question is asked of a set containing the dead-letter box itself.
obs And the pen found it is wrong in BOTH directions, which I had not expected. A record STAGED when the lap died rides in `^2` and reads as history: false safe. A record merely UNTRACKED rides in `^3`, which the default walk prunes, so `--all` reads nothing: right answer, wrong reason, and it would read nothing for a landed record parked the same way. Same probe, opposite answers, neither about landing. Both are reproduced in the control now (legs 6-8) rather than asserted in the header, so a future git that changes either says so on the lap it changes.
obs The design decision I would defend hardest: the reading is about the RECORD, never the box. A stash reads clean once its log has landed and stays standing. That means the only way to turn this guard green is to put a record back in the channel -- a meter a hand could satisfy by dropping evidence is a meter that teaches deletion. Proven in the pen by landing a record and leaving its stash exactly where it stood.
obs I ran `fleet_round_open.sh` mid-lap to test the wiring and it stashed my own uncommitted work -- the scan, the control, the witness. The report line then stayed silent, because step 3 had carried the scan away before step 5 could read it. Popped back byte-for-byte. The lesson is in the code: the call is guarded by the scan's own presence, and a round-open must never be able to fail on its own report.
obs Grade what you touch pushed one frame: `fleet_round_open.sh` reads C/74, and the HEAD blob read exactly 74 before I touched it, so the grade is the elder header's. Molted in place under a cairn -- six sentences whose negative form was incidental, claims held exact. It reached C+/75, and then the accounting settled it: of the 11 negatives remaining, three are this tree's own seated nouns (`dead lap`, `dead-letter box`, `NEVER MERGE`) and eight are the four failure states the script exists to tell apart. Not one is incidental. A frame closed with a number is a legitimate answer, and this one is also evidence for the register question already standing on the card.
obs The row's other half is answered rather than left: its 29 unreviewed files dedupe to 26 distinct, 23 stand in the worktree, and 3 are superseded rather than lost -- a REDS fold shelf a peer published differently, and two draft fixtures the landed `%446` close named out of existence, referenced by nothing the tree carries.
obs Reds first kept firing after the work was done, and both catches came from the HOT pass rather than the cold one. `%470`: `readme_reach` read `broken_in_living=1` -- a cairn in CHECKPOINTS.md whose own sentence says *a published fold does not move* linking to the fold name that did not survive, since the peer who actually published `%462` named the shelf otherwise. `reds_fold` reads `readme_reach`'s number, so one dead link took two guards. It stood in HEAD, and the last COLD pass at `053014` read 0 red, so it landed after that and stood across the fleet for two hours.
obs `%471` is the sharper one, and it is this lap's own shape a third time: an instrument reading a set that contains itself. With `%470` repaired the pass still refused, 133 run, 129 green, 3 gated, ONE red -- and the one was `standing_equipment`. The runner appends each verdict to `standing-equipment-runs.kyri` AFTER running the guard, and that guard is in the roster it reads, so its own red returns next pass as the cause of its own red. I proved it rather than reasoned it: lifting its own row gave `runs_red=0 verdict=ok`, putting it back gave `roster_broken`, nothing else changed.
obs What makes `%471` worse than an ordinary latch is where the state lives. `standing-equipment-runs.kyri` is untracked on purpose, one per pier, so a ship stuck this way is invisible from every other tree and from this tree's own history. The repair skips the row by NAME through `STANDING_SELF_GUARD` -- so the pen plants the latch under its own name rather than trusting where the row sits -- and REPORTS it as `runs_self_row`, because a silent skip cannot be told from a scan that stopped reading the record. Eight pen legs, and the ones I care about most are the negative ones: a peer's red in the same record still refuses, is counted once, and is named.
obs Three instruments in one lap read a set containing themselves -- `git log --all` reaching `refs/stash`, `reds_fold` inheriting `readme_reach`'s verdict, and `standing_equipment` reading its own row. The first two I found by building; the third found me, by refusing a pass whose every other guard was green.
obs The card cost more care than the code. It stood at 40,954 of 40,960 with six bytes free, so this round's front took a cairn and three folds of my OWN landed lines -- `%451`/`%469` to a pointer, the `%450` pair merged, and my two register findings merged into one paragraph, since they were always one ask. No peer's line was touched. The card lands at exactly 40,960.
